Package: omx-clap-host
Architecture: any
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Recommends: liblilv-0-0
Description: use CLAP and LV2 plugins on a JACK rig, driven like mod-host
 omx-clap-host hosts CLAP and LV2 plugins in a live JACK or PipeWire rig.
 Each plugin runs as a JACK client with its own ports, and an instrument
 gets a MIDI input as well. Anything that already controls mod-host can
 control it over the same socket with the same commands. An LV2 plugin,
 added by its URI, runs natively: a real LV2 instance loaded through lilv,
 with no emulation and no wrapper process. The LV2 adapter linked into the
 host maps the host interface, presenting the plugin's ports and controls
 as CLAP ports and parameters, and loads lilv when the first one is added.
 .
 It comes with omx-clap-scan, which lists what is in a CLAP bundle and
 survives a plugin that crashes while loading.

Package: omx-clap-host-tests
Architecture: any
Depends: omx-clap-host (= ${binary:Version}), python3, ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: fixtures to test a program that drives omx-clap-host
 Plugins and a signal source for testing a program that runs omx-clap-host as
 its CLAP worker: a plugin that reads the track information the host passes
 it, a compressor that reports meters, a gain that aborts on a write of 0.9
 or more, a JACK client that plays a constant level, and one that plays a
 noise into up to six effects and records each of them from the same cycles.
 They are installed in /usr/libexec/omx-clap-host/tests, away from the
 directories CLAP hosts scan, and are of no use to anyone else.
 socket_conformance.py, beside them, checks how any server of the mod-host
 socket protocol splits what it receives into commands.

Package: libomx-clap-core1
Section: libs
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Pre-Depends: ${misc:Pre-Depends}
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}
Description: engine that runs CLAP plugins for omx-clap-host
 The library that loads and runs CLAP plugins: it checks a plugin before using
 it, activates and warms it up, keeps its parameters and saved state, and runs
 its audio on the thread of the program that hosts it. It knows nothing about
 JACK or sockets, so a program of your own can host CLAP plugins on it as
 well.

Package: libomx-clap-core-dev
Section: libdevel
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Depends: libomx-contract-dev (>= 3.1.0), ${misc:Depends}
Description: build a program that hosts CLAP plugins on libomx-clap-core
 What you need to write a program that hosts CLAP plugins on libomx-clap-core:
 the headers, the pkg-config file, the list of functions the library
 promises to keep, with the baseline each release is checked against, and
 the hosting limits as definitions in omx-contract's contract directory.

Package: libomx-clap-lv2-dev
Section: libdevel
Architecture: any
Multi-Arch: same
Depends: libomx-clap-core-dev (= ${binary:Version}), clap-headers, pkgconf, ${misc:Depends}
Description: LV2 adapter of omx-clap-host, as a static library to link
 libomx-clap-lv2 is the LV2 adapter omx-clap-host links in, for a program of
 your own that hosts plugins on libomx-clap-core: it loads an LV2 plugin
 through lilv as a real LV2 instance and maps the host interface, presenting
 the plugin's ports and controls as CLAP ports and parameters, with its
 latency, its own background worker and its default settings. It is a
 static library with its header and pkg-config file, linked at build time,
 and it loads lilv when the first LV2 bundle is opened, so a program that
 never opens one never needs lilv. LV2 at run time is omx-clap-host with
 liblilv-0-0, which plugin-hostd also runs as the worker of an LV2 plugin.
